00:37:40.242 --> 00:37:41.710
Now, wait a second.

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:44.346
I'm very excited.

00:37:44.346 --> 00:37:46.081
Oh wait, there was
a lot of noise.

00:37:51.019 --> 00:37:51.920
I tried.

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:53.088
I got to work on my arm.

00:37:57.926 --> 00:38:01.029
It's such a beautiful material.

00:38:01.029 --> 00:38:02.430
Too excited.

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:03.098
We'll have more.

00:38:08.503 --> 00:38:17.479
Now I don't mean to
bring us down after why

00:38:17.479 --> 00:38:18.980
this matters and excitement.

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:22.017
But here's the thing,
if there's one thing

00:38:22.017 --> 00:38:23.585
you know about
chemistry by now, it's

00:38:23.585 --> 00:38:26.488
that chemistry lives
in the fast lane.

00:38:26.488 --> 00:38:28.723
And what I mean is, they
know about the rules.

00:38:28.723 --> 00:38:31.760
And they also know they're
meant to be broken.

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:36.031
And Lewis and octets
are no exception.

00:38:36.031 --> 00:38:42.037
And so there are exceptions
to Lewis's octet rule.

00:38:42.037 --> 00:38:44.739
And I'll give you a
couple of examples.

00:38:44.739 --> 00:38:46.274
And it has to do
with the concepts

00:38:46.274 --> 00:38:51.880
that we've been talking about,
but in particular, like boron.

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:57.285
Boron trifluoride, well,
that one, you could draw it.

00:38:57.285 --> 00:39:01.890
If you wanted to, you
could draw it like this.

00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:10.498
And let's see, here, F,
and you can do this, F.

00:39:10.498 --> 00:39:11.966
But the problem
with this is then,

00:39:11.966 --> 00:39:14.336
when you look at the formal
charges, you've got a minus 1

00:39:14.336 --> 00:39:16.971
there and a minus 1 there.

00:39:16.971 --> 00:39:20.975
And so actually, what
this structure does,

00:39:20.975 --> 00:39:24.846
is boron is like, you
know what, I'm flexible.

00:39:24.846 --> 00:39:27.649
I don't need my eight.

00:39:27.649 --> 00:39:29.984
I can be OK with six.

00:39:29.984 --> 00:39:32.620
As long as you keep
sharing, fluorine atoms,

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:36.024
as long as you keep
sharing those electrons

00:39:36.024 --> 00:39:40.862
so we can make these three
covalent bonds all equivalent,

00:39:40.862 --> 00:39:43.498
I'm OK.

00:39:43.498 --> 00:39:46.134
And so this is actually--
and you can see,

00:39:46.134 --> 00:39:49.304
the formal charges on all
the atoms here is zero.

00:39:49.304 --> 00:39:53.174
This is actually the
stable structure,

00:39:53.174 --> 00:39:55.410
even though boron does
not, this does not

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:58.313
satisfy the octet rule.

00:39:58.313 --> 00:40:02.083
Chemistry, breaking the rules.

00:40:02.083 --> 00:40:04.753
So boron is said to be happy.

00:40:04.753 --> 00:40:11.693
Boron can be, what is called in
this world, electron deficient.

00:40:15.797 --> 00:40:19.033
And by the same token,
you can go the other way.

00:40:19.033 --> 00:40:23.671
And sulfur likes to go the other
way, because sometimes sulfur,

00:40:23.671 --> 00:40:25.273
so there we have
sulfur tetrafluoride.

00:40:25.273 --> 00:40:27.475
So I'm using the
same external atom,

00:40:27.475 --> 00:40:35.750
but sulfur also prefers
to look like this

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:40.088
and to have one extra
lone pair there.

00:40:40.088 --> 00:40:47.028
And now we can go like this,
and like that, and like that.

00:40:47.028 --> 00:40:52.801
And so what sulfur will
do, oh boy, lots of dots,

00:40:52.801 --> 00:40:56.037
and so what sulfur
will do is it will

00:40:56.037 --> 00:41:05.213
form what's called an expanded
octet, expanded octet.

00:41:05.213 --> 00:41:08.416
And this will become
also, a little bit--

00:41:08.416 --> 00:41:10.585
we'll sort of understand
this also a little bit more

00:41:10.585 --> 00:41:11.986
once we look at shapes.

00:41:11.986 --> 00:41:13.721
Shapes are going to
play an important role

00:41:13.721 --> 00:41:16.558
in molecular stability.

00:41:16.558 --> 00:41:18.626
But for now, I just wanted
you to know that there

00:41:18.626 --> 00:41:21.963
are exceptions to the rules.

00:41:21.963 --> 00:41:24.132
And in particular, as you
go beyond the first couple

00:41:24.132 --> 00:41:28.403
of rows, so you go down
in the period table,

00:41:28.403 --> 00:41:31.339
you do find atoms more
willing, like sulfur,

00:41:31.339 --> 00:41:35.743
to have an expanded octet
in order to reach the lowest

00:41:35.743 --> 00:41:37.278
energy state.

00:41:37.278 --> 00:41:41.149
Now, in the last two
minutes, I come back to this.

00:41:41.149 --> 00:41:43.284
Let's just bring
it all back home

00:41:43.284 --> 00:41:46.988
around Lewis, and around
electronegativity,

00:41:46.988 --> 00:41:52.060
and around all these
covalent bonding concepts.

00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:53.428
And what I want
to leave you with

00:41:53.428 --> 00:41:56.130
is what was kind
of the point, part

00:41:56.130 --> 00:41:58.433
of the point of your goody bag.

00:41:58.433 --> 00:42:01.035
Which is, I wanted you to get
your hands on these materials

00:42:01.035 --> 00:42:04.606
so you could touch and
feel the differences

00:42:04.606 --> 00:42:10.178
and see that actually it's
the properties at the end that

00:42:10.178 --> 00:42:13.982
are also very important for
determining what a material is.

00:42:13.982 --> 00:42:19.420
So you can make rules like
electronegativity difference

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:24.025
less than 1.6 is going
to be polar covalent

00:42:24.025 --> 00:42:25.894
or non-polar covalent,
or greater than two,

00:42:25.894 --> 00:42:27.395
it's probably ionic.

00:42:27.395 --> 00:42:29.764
But at the end of
the day, you've

00:42:29.764 --> 00:42:31.332
got to come back to properties.

00:42:37.605 --> 00:42:39.774
I'll put up a
slide showing this,

00:42:39.774 --> 00:42:41.442
but ionic solids
are hard, brittle,

00:42:41.442 --> 00:42:43.244
and solid at room temperature.

00:42:43.244 --> 00:42:47.815
Covalent solids can be
gases, liquids, or solids.

00:42:47.815 --> 00:42:50.018
Covalent solids have
a low melting point.

00:42:50.018 --> 00:42:52.854
Ionic solids are typically high.

00:42:52.854 --> 00:42:54.923
Covalent solids are
poor conductors.

00:42:54.923 --> 00:42:59.060
Ionic ones are usually
insulators as a solid.

00:42:59.060 --> 00:43:00.962
This is what you
need to also do.

00:43:00.962 --> 00:43:04.132
You can't just go by one metric.

00:43:04.132 --> 00:43:05.833
You got to look
at the properties.

00:43:05.833 --> 00:43:07.168
It's what Mendeleev did.

00:43:07.168 --> 00:43:10.204
And it's what chemists have
always done to make progress.

00:43:10.204 --> 00:43:13.575
OK so, on that note,
